\section{Where statsalike departs from Stata}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{No prefixes i. and c.} Whether a variable enters a model as a factor
or as a numeric variable follows from its type in R. Factors and
character variables are categorical, numeric variables (also labeled
variables from haven) are continuous unless they are named in
\code{categorical}. Interactions are therefore written without prefixes,
\code{"x##g"} instead of Stata's \code{"c.x##i.g"}. The prefixes \code{i.}, \code{c.} and
\code{ib#.} are still understood. Output labels follow the same rule, the
interaction of \code{wt} and the factor \code{am} is labeled \code{am#wt}, not
\code{am#c.wt}.
\item \strong{Which types are numeric.} As Stata's \code{c.} count all numeric storage
types that \code{\link[=s.describe]{s.describe()}} shows: \code{dbl} (double), \code{int} (integer), \code{lgl}
(logical, \code{TRUE} = 1) and numeric variables with value labels (haven's
\code{dbl+lbl}, \code{int+lbl}). As Stata's \code{i.} count \code{fct} (factor), \code{ord}
(ordered factor) and \code{chr} (character). Dates (\code{date}, class \code{Date}),
times (\code{dttm}, \code{POSIXct}) and time differences (\code{drtn}, \code{difftime}) enter as
numbers: R stores a date as days and a time as seconds since 1970-01-01,
whereas Stata counts days (\verb{\%td}) and milliseconds (\verb{\%tc}) since
1960-01-01. The coefficient of a date is therefore the same as in Stata,
but the constant refers to another day (1970-01-01). A note below the
output names the unit and the origin. For an interpretable constant,
subtract a reference date first, e.g.
\code{mutate(days = as.numeric(date - as.Date("2020-01-01")))}.
\item \strong{Hint for labeled numeric variables.} Variables imported from Stata or
SPSS with value labels (e.g. education coded 1 to 5) are numeric in R and
enter as continuous variables. If at least two labeled values occur, the
estimation commands show a hint (once per variable and session) how to
enter the variable as a factor (\code{categorical = x} or
\code{haven::as_factor()}). Variables given with the prefix \code{c.} get no hint,
\code{options(statsalike.labelled_hint = FALSE)} turns the hint off.
\item \strong{Notes on interactions.} Below the output of a model with
interactions, and below \code{\link[=s.etable]{s.etable()}}, a note tells how every variable
enters the interaction, e.g. "cyl#wt = cyl (factor) x wt (numeric)".
Stata has no such note. \code{options(statsalike.stata_notation = TRUE)} brings
back Stata's labels and drops the notes.
\item \strong{Average marginal effects in the estimation command.} \code{ame = TRUE} in
\code{\link[=s.regress]{s.regress()}}, \code{\link[=s.logit]{s.logit()}}, \code{\link[=s.probit]{s.probit()}} and the other estimation
commands shows the average marginal effects of all regressors (Stata's
\verb{margins, dydx(*)}) in place of the coefficients. Stata needs a separate
\code{margins} call. The result can be shown with \code{\link[=s.marginsplot]{s.marginsplot()}} and in
\code{\link[=s.etable]{s.etable()}}, e.g. next to the coefficients and odds ratios of the same
model.
\item \strong{Tjur's R-squared.} After \code{\link[=s.logit]{s.logit()}} and \code{\link[=s.probit]{s.probit()}}, \code{\link[=s.ereturn]{s.ereturn()}}
returns Tjur's coefficient of discrimination (\code{r2_t}), the mean predicted
probability of the cases with the outcome minus that of the cases
without it. \code{\link[=s.etable]{s.etable()}} shows it with \code{mstat = "r2_t"}. Stata does not
report it. It equals \code{performance::r2_tjur()}.
\item \strong{Graphs} use the default design of ggplot2, not Stata's graph scheme.
\item \strong{Tables} (\code{\link[=s.etable]{s.etable()}}) follow the \code{etable} command of Stata 17, all
other output the layout of Stata 15.
}
}

\section{Specifying the model}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Categorical variables.} Factors and character variables enter the
model as indicator variables with the first category as base, as with
R's treatment contrasts. \code{base = c(x = 3)} sets another base category as
\code{stats::relevel()} does in R.
\item \strong{Interactions} \code{"x##g"} correspond to \code{x * g} in an R formula, \code{"x#g"}
to \code{x:g} and \code{"x#x"} (numeric \code{x}) to \code{I(x^2)}. As in Stata, base
categories are not displayed. Combinations of categories without
observations are shown as \code{(empty)} (R gives \code{NA}).
\item \strong{Collinear variables.} \code{\link[=s.regress]{s.regress()}} omits collinear variables as
Stata's \code{regress} does. Of two collinear variables it can drop a
different one than \code{\link[stats:lm]{stats::lm()}}, which always sets the later variable
in the formula to \code{NA} (of \code{wt} and \code{2 * wt} Stata drops \code{wt}). The
maximum-likelihood commands drop the later variable, as R does. Omitted
variables are listed as \code{(omitted)} with a note.
\item \strong{Perfect prediction.} If a variable predicts the outcome of a logit or
probit model perfectly for some of its values, \code{\link[=s.logit]{s.logit()}} and
\code{\link[=s.probit]{s.probit()}} drop the variable and these observations, with Stata's note
(e.g. "1.rep78 != 0 predicts failure perfectly"). \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} keeps
them, warns "fitted probabilities numerically 0 or 1 occurred" and
reports huge coefficients and standard errors. In the case of complete
separation statsalike stops with an error.
\item \strong{Missing values} are excluded listwise, as with \code{na.omit}. The number
of observations is shown in the header (\verb{Number of obs}). Models with
different variables are therefore often estimated on different samples.
}
}

\section{Estimation}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Algorithm.} The maximum-likelihood models are fitted with Newton-
Raphson steps and the step rules and convergence criteria of Stata's \code{ml}
(relative change of the coefficients at most 1e-6 or of the log
likelihood at most 1e-7, and a scaled gradient below 1e-5), and the
iteration log is printed. \code{\link[stats:glm]{stats::glm()}} uses iteratively reweighted
least squares, \code{MASS::polr()}, \code{nnet::multinom()} and
\code{survival::survreg()} use other optimizers. The estimates agree to many
digits with those of \code{glm()}. Those of functions that stop earlier or use
a numerical Hessian can differ in the last digits.
\item \strong{Probit.} \code{\link[=s.probit]{s.probit()}} gives the coefficients of
\code{glm(family = binomial(link = "probit"))}. Its standard errors use the
observed information, as Stata does, \code{glm()} the expected information.
For the probit link the two differ slightly (for the logit link they are
the same). \code{\link[=s.oprobit]{s.oprobit()}} corresponds to \code{MASS::polr(method = "probit")}.
\item \strong{Base outcome of the multinomial logit.} \code{\link[=s.mlogit]{s.mlogit()}} uses the most
frequent outcome as base, as Stata does, \code{nnet::multinom()} the first
level. Use \code{baseoutcome} to choose it.
\item \strong{Ordered models.} \code{\link[=s.ologit]{s.ologit()}} and \code{\link[=s.oprobit]{s.oprobit()}} have no constant but
cut points (\verb{/cut1}, \verb{/cut2}, ...), with P(y <= j) = F(cut_j - xb). This
is the parametrization of \code{MASS::polr()} (\code{zeta}), but other R functions
(e.g. some with \code{+ xb}) reverse the sign of the coefficients.
\item \strong{Poisson regression.} \code{exposure = t} corresponds to
\code{offset(log(t))} in an R formula.
\item \strong{Negative binomial regression.} \code{\link[=s.nbreg]{s.nbreg()}} reports the dispersion as
\code{alpha} (and \verb{/lnalpha}), the variance being mu + alpha * mu^2.
\code{MASS::glm.nb()} reports \code{theta} = 1 / alpha. The coefficients agree, the
standard errors of \code{glm.nb()} treat theta as known and can differ in the
last digits. The likelihood-ratio test of alpha = 0 lies on the boundary
of the parameter space and is therefore tested against a mixture of
chi-squared distributions (\code{chibar2(01)}, half the usual p-value).
\item \strong{Tobit regression.} \code{\link[=s.tobit]{s.tobit()}} reports the variance of the error
(\code{var(e.y)}, as Stata 15), \code{survival::survreg(dist = "gaussian")} the log
of its standard deviation (\code{Log(scale)}). As in Stata, the tests of the
coefficients are t tests with N - k degrees of freedom, \code{survreg()} uses
z tests. Stata 15 refines its starting values with a grid search that
statsalike does not reproduce. The iteration log can therefore differ and
the estimates can differ in the seventh digit, where both stop.
\item \strong{Model test and fit.} The ML commands report the likelihood-ratio test
against the model with the constant only (\verb{LR chi2}, with robust or
cluster-robust standard errors a Wald test, \verb{Wald chi2}) and McFadden's
pseudo R-squared, 1 - ll / ll_0. \code{summary.glm()} reports deviances
instead. The LR statistic is the difference of null and residual deviance.
\item \strong{Weights.} Frequency weights (\code{fweight}) replicate observations, so N
and the degrees of freedom are the sum of the weights. \code{lm(weights = )}
treats weights as precision weights and gives wrong standard errors for
frequency weights. Analytic weights (\code{aweight}) give the coefficients and
standard errors of \code{lm(weights = )}. Sampling weights (\code{pweight}) always
imply robust standard errors (see \link{statsalike-weights}).
\item \strong{Standardized coefficients} (\code{beta = TRUE}) use the standard deviations
of the estimation sample (weighted, if weights are given).
}
}

\section{Standard errors, confidence intervals and tests}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Robust standard errors} after \code{\link[=s.regress]{s.regress()}} are HC1, i.e. HC0 times
N / (N - k). The default of \code{sandwich::vcovHC()} is HC3. Stata's result is
\code{sandwich::vcovHC(m, type = "HC1")}. \code{vce = "hc2"} and \code{vce = "hc3"} give
\code{type = "HC2"} and \code{type = "HC3"}.
\item \strong{Robust standard errors of the ML models} are HC0 times N / (N - 1).
\code{sandwich::vcovHC(m, type = "HC0")} lacks this factor, \code{type = "HC1"}
uses N / (N - k) instead.
\item \strong{Cluster-robust standard errors} use the factor
G / (G - 1) * (N - 1) / (N - k) after regress and G / (G - 1) after the ML
models (G = number of clusters). After regress these are the standard
errors of \code{sandwich::vcovCL(m, cluster = ~ c, type = "HC1")}. After
regress, t tests, confidence intervals and the F test use G - 1 degrees
of freedom, not N - k as \code{lmtest::coeftest()} does by default (pass
\code{df = G - 1} to get Stata's p-values). With fewer clusters than
coefficients the overall F test cannot be computed and is shown as \code{.}.
HC2 and HC3 with clusters (added in Stata 18) are not available.
\item \strong{Confidence intervals} of the ML models are Wald intervals, b +/- z *
SE. \code{confint()} for a \code{glm()} uses the profile likelihood and gives
(slightly) different, asymmetric intervals. \code{confint.default()} gives the
Wald intervals. After regress the intervals are those of \code{confint.lm()}.
\item \strong{Odds ratios, relative risk ratios and incidence-rate ratios}
(\code{or}, \code{rrr}, \code{irr}, \code{eform}) are exp(b). Their standard errors are
exp(b) * SE (delta method), the confidence limits exp() of those of b, and
z and p are the ones of the coefficient.
\item \strong{Tests of coefficients} (\code{\link[=s.test]{s.test()}}) are Wald tests, F tests with the
residual degrees of freedom after regress and tobit, chi-squared tests
after the other ML models. For nested linear models estimated on the same
sample the F test equals the one of \code{anova(m1, m2)}. \code{anova()} for glm
models gives the likelihood-ratio test, which is \code{\link[=s.lrtest]{s.lrtest()}}.
\item \strong{Linear combinations} (\code{\link[=s.lincom]{s.lincom()}}) correspond to
\code{car::linearHypothesis()} or \code{multcomp::glht()}, with the t distribution
after regress and the normal distribution after the ML models.
\item \strong{Contrasts} (\code{\link[=s.contrast]{s.contrast()}}) are contrasts of the margins of the
linear prediction with all other factor variables weighted equally
(Stata's \code{asbalanced}), as \code{emmeans::contrast()} with
\code{emmeans(m, ~ x)}. The joint test of a factor variable equals the
Type III test of \code{car::Anova(type = 3)} with sum-to-zero contrasts, so
it differs from R's \code{anova()} (Type I) in models with interactions or
unbalanced data. The operators correspond to R's coding schemes: \code{r.} to
\code{contr.treatment}, \code{g.} to deviations from the grand mean
(\code{contr.sum}), \code{ar.} to \code{MASS::contr.sdif}, \code{j.} (up to scaling) to
\code{contr.helmert}, \code{h.} to Helmert contrasts in the other direction and
\code{p.} to \code{contr.poly} (orthonormal polynomials). The p-values are not adjusted for multiple comparisons.
\item \strong{Likelihood-ratio test.} \code{\link[=s.lrtest]{s.lrtest()}} refuses models estimated on
different numbers of observations, as Stata does. Restrict both models to
the same cases first (e.g. with \code{tidyr::drop_na()} on all variables of
the larger model).
}
}

\section{Tests of means and scales}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{t test} (\code{\link[=s.ttest]{s.ttest()}}). Stata assumes equal variances in the
two-sample test, \code{t.test()} does not (\code{var.equal = FALSE} is R's
default). Stata's test is \code{t.test(var.equal = TRUE)}, \code{unequal = TRUE}
gives R's default (Satterthwaite's degrees of freedom). \code{welch = TRUE}
uses Welch's formula for the degrees of freedom, which \code{t.test()} does
not offer.
\item \strong{One-way analysis of variance} (\code{\link[=s.oneway]{s.oneway()}}) gives the F test of
\code{anova(lm(y ~ g))} and Bartlett's test of \code{bartlett.test()}. The
Bonferroni comparisons equal \code{pairwise.t.test(p.adjust.method = "bonferroni")} with the pooled standard deviation. Scheffé and Šidák
comparisons are not in base R.
\item \strong{Analysis of variance} (\code{\link[=s.anova]{s.anova()}}) reports partial (Type III) sums of
squares, as Stata does. \code{anova()} in R gives sequential (Type I) sums of
squares, which agree only for the last term or in balanced designs.
\code{car::Anova(type = 3)} with sum-to-zero contrasts (\code{contr.sum}) gives
Stata's table.
\item \strong{Cronbach's alpha} (\code{\link[=s.alpha]{s.alpha()}}) reverses items that load negatively
on the first principal component of the items, as Stata does, unless
\code{asis = TRUE}.
\code{psych::alpha()} only warns and needs \code{check.keys = TRUE}. The default is
the unstandardized alpha (from the covariances), \code{std = TRUE} gives the
standardized alpha (from the correlations).
}
}

\section{Margins}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Predictive margins and average marginal effects} (\code{\link[=s.margins]{s.margins()}})
average over the observations of the estimation sample. The variable of
interest is set to a value for everyone, the other variables keep their
observed values. \code{ggeffects::ggpredict()} instead sets the other
variables to their means and factors to their reference category
(predictions for a "typical" case). Its results are therefore closer to
\code{atmeans = TRUE}, but not identical (see the next point). The approach of
\code{\link[=s.margins]{s.margins()}} is the one of \code{marginaleffects::avg_predictions()} with
\code{variables} (counterfactual values) and \code{marginaleffects::avg_slopes()}.
\code{by} in marginaleffects instead averages within the observed groups.
\item \strong{\code{atmeans}} sets continuous variables to their means and factor
variables to the shares of their categories in the sample (e.g. 0.47 for
women), not to a reference category as \code{ggpredict()} does, nor to equal
weights for all categories as \code{emmeans::emmeans()} does by default. As
in Stata, the predictions are evaluated at the means in single precision
(float), which can change the seventh digit.
\item \strong{\code{dydx} for categorical variables.} For a factor the marginal effect is
the discrete change from the base category. For a numeric 0/1 variable it
is the derivative. The output notes which one is shown.
\item \strong{Pairwise comparisons} (\code{pwcompare}) are the differences of all pairs
of margins without adjustment for multiple comparisons, as
\code{emmeans::pairs(adjust = "none")} (with the sign reversed, Stata
subtracts the earlier category from the later one).
\item \strong{Standard errors} are delta-method standard errors. statsalike uses
analytic derivatives, Stata numerical ones, so in rare cases the last
displayed digit can differ. After regress and tobit the tests use the t
distribution with the residual degrees of freedom, otherwise the normal
distribution.
\item \strong{\code{at} with several variables} uses all combinations of the values,
with the first variable varying slowest.
}
}

\section{Predictions and regression diagnostics}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Default of \code{\link[=s.predict]{s.predict()}}.} After \code{\link[=s.logit]{s.logit()}} and \code{\link[=s.probit]{s.probit()}} it gives
the predicted probability, after \code{\link[=s.poisson]{s.poisson()}} and \code{\link[=s.nbreg]{s.nbreg()}} the
predicted number of events. \code{predict.glm()} gives the linear prediction
(log odds, log count) unless \code{type = "response"}.
\item \strong{Out-of-sample predictions.} Fitted values and probabilities are
computed for every observation with nonmissing regressors, also for
observations outside the estimation sample (e.g. with a missing dependent
variable). \code{predict(m)} in R only gives the estimation sample (with
\code{na.action = na.exclude} padded with \code{NA}).
\item \strong{Breusch-Pagan test} (\code{\link[=s.estat]{s.estat()}} \code{"hettest"}). Stata's default is the
version that assumes normal errors, with the fitted values as the only
variable. \code{lmtest::bptest()} by default uses all regressors and the
studentized (Koenker) version. Stata's default corresponds to
\code{lmtest::bptest(m, ~ yhat, data = transform(df, yhat = fitted(m)), studentize = FALSE)}. \code{iid = TRUE} gives the studentized version
(\code{studentize = TRUE}), \code{rhs = TRUE} uses the regressors of the model
(\code{lmtest::bptest(m, studentize = FALSE)}).
\item \strong{RESET test} (\code{"ovtest"}) uses the second to fourth powers of the
fitted values. \code{lmtest::resettest()} by default only uses the second and
third (\code{lmtest::resettest(m, power = 2:4)} gives Stata's test).
\item \strong{Specification link test} (\code{\link[=s.linktest]{s.linktest()}}) regresses the outcome on
the prediction and its square, as Stata does. There is no direct
counterpart in base R.
\item \strong{Variance inflation factors} (\code{"vif"}) are given for every coefficient,
also for every category of a factor variable. \code{car::vif()} reports one
generalized VIF (GVIF) per factor.
\item \strong{Information criteria} (\code{"ic"}) count the coefficients only. For a
linear regression \code{AIC()} and \code{BIC()} in R also count the residual
variance as a parameter, so R's values are larger by 2 (AIC) and by
log(N) (BIC). The log likelihood is the same. For the ML models the
values agree with R. The corrected criteria AICc and CAIC of Stata 18 are
not available.
\item \strong{Hosmer-Lemeshow test} (\code{"gof"} with \code{group}). The groups are formed by
the quantiles of the predicted probabilities as in Stata, with tied
probabilities kept in one group. \code{ResourceSelection::hoslem.test()} cuts
the groups differently, so the statistic can differ. Without \code{group} the
Pearson test is computed over covariate patterns, not over observations.
\item \strong{Classification table} (\code{"classification"}). An observation counts as
positive if its predicted probability is at least the cutoff (0.5).
\item \strong{ROC curve} (\code{\link[=s.lroc]{s.lroc()}}). The area under the curve equals the
Mann-Whitney statistic, e.g. \code{pROC::auc()}.
\item \strong{Added-variable and component-plus-residual plots} (\code{\link[=s.avplots]{s.avplots()}},
\code{\link[=s.cprplot]{s.cprplot()}}) correspond to \code{car::avPlots()} and \code{car::crPlots()}. Unlike
\code{crPlots()}, \code{\link[=s.cprplot]{s.cprplot()}} adds a smooth line only with \code{lowess = TRUE}.
\item \strong{Normal quantile plot} (\code{\link[=s.qnorm]{s.qnorm()}}) plots the sorted values against
the normal quantiles at i / (N + 1), with the mean and standard deviation
of the variable. \code{qqnorm()} uses \code{ppoints()} and \code{qqline()} a line through
the quartiles.
\item \strong{Kernel density} (\code{\link[=s.kdensity]{s.kdensity()}}) uses Stata's Epanechnikov kernel,
the bandwidth 0.9 * min(SD, IQR / 1.349) * N^(-1/5) and 50 points.
\code{density()} uses a Gaussian kernel by default.
\item \strong{Standardized and studentized residuals, leverage and Cook's distance}
agree with \code{rstandard()}, \code{rstudent()}, \code{hatvalues()} and
\code{cooks.distance()}.
}
}

\section{Display}{

\itemize{
\item Numbers use Stata's formats (\verb{\%9.0g} etc., without a leading zero,
\code{.123}). p-values have three decimals, so 0.000 means p < 0.0005.
\item Long variable names are abbreviated with \code{~} (\code{arbeitszeit~e}), long
value labels end in \code{..}, and labels of interactions are broken over
several lines, as in Stata.
\item Statistics that cannot be computed are shown as \code{.}.
}
}

\section{Display formats}{

Stata stores with every variable a \emph{display format} that says how its
values are printed, and its table commands take a format for their
numbers (\code{nformat()}). statsalike uses the same notation:
\itemize{
\item in \code{nformat} of \code{\link[=s.table]{s.table()}} and \code{\link[=s.etable]{s.etable()}} (Stata's \code{nformat()}),
for all statistics (\code{nformat = "\%9.2f"}) or per statistic
(\code{nformat = c(mean = "\%9.2f", sd = "\%9.3f")}, \code{\link[=s.table]{s.table()}} only),
\item in \code{\link[=s.describe]{s.describe()}}, which lists the format of every variable
(\verb{Display format}): the format stored in a Stata file (haven keeps it as
the attribute \code{format.stata}), otherwise Stata's default, \verb{\%10.0g} for
numbers (double), \verb{\%8.0g} for integers, logical variables and factors,
\verb{\%td} for dates, \verb{\%tc} for times and \verb{\%-#s} (left-aligned, # characters)
for strings,
\item in \code{format = TRUE} of \code{\link[=s.summarize]{s.summarize()}}, which shows the mean, standard
deviation, minimum and maximum in the format of each variable, as
Stata's \verb{summarize, format}.
}

A numeric format reads \verb{\%} \emph{width} \code{.} \emph{digits} \emph{type} (\code{c}):
\itemize{
\item \strong{Type} \code{f} (fixed) shows exactly \emph{digits} decimals: \verb{\%9.2f} gives
1234.57 and 0.01, \verb{\%9.0f} gives 1235 and 0.
\item \strong{Type} \code{g} (general) chooses between fixed and exponential notation.
With \code{.0} (\verb{\%9.0g}, \verb{\%8.0g}, \verb{\%10.0g}, Stata's defaults) it shows up to
seven significant digits and drops trailing zeros: 1234.568, 0.0123,
42. With digits greater than zero it shows that many significant
digits: \verb{\%9.3g} gives 1.23e+03 and 0.0123.
\item \strong{Type} \code{e} (exponential) shows \emph{digits} decimals of the mantissa:
\verb{\%10.3e} gives 1.235e+03.
\item \strong{Suffix} \code{c} adds thousands separators: \verb{\%12.2fc} gives 1,234.57.
\item \strong{Width} (the 9 in \verb{\%9.2f}) is the number of characters that Stata
reserves for the column. statsalike sizes every column to its content, so
the width (and the \code{-} for left alignment) is accepted but has no effect.
}

Formats only change what is printed and exported. The results (tibbles)
keep the values in full precision. Any other \code{sprintf()} format works as
well, e.g. \code{"\%.1f\%\%"}. Unlike Stata, the tables of \code{\link[=s.table]{s.table()}} and
\code{\link[=s.etable]{s.etable()}} keep the leading zero of numbers between -1 and 1 (0.5, not
.5); the output of the estimation commands follows Stata.

Without \code{nformat}, \code{\link[=s.table]{s.table()}} shows frequencies as whole numbers,
percentages with two decimals, proportions with four and all other
statistics in the general format \verb{\%9.0g}, as Stata does. \code{\link[=s.etable]{s.etable()}}
uses \verb{\%9.3f}.
}

\examples{
m <- s.regress(mtcars, mpg, wt, hp, vce = "robust")
# the same standard errors in R
# sandwich::vcovHC(lm(mpg ~ wt + hp, mtcars), type = "HC1")

l <- s.logit(mtcars, am, wt, nolog = TRUE)
d <- s.predict(mtcars, l, "p")   # probabilities, as predict(glm, type = "response")

